The real power of Spring's stereotype model is that you can **compose your own**. Any annotation that is itself annotated with `@Component` (or any of its specializations) becomes a stereotype that component-scan discovers.

This is useful for encoding architecture rules. Instead of relying on developers to remember 'use `@Service` for business logic and `@Repository` for data access,' you create domain-specific stereotypes that carry those rules.

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Service  // inherits @Component behavior
@Transactional  // every bean gets transaction management
public @interface UseCase {}

// Usage — clear intent, automatic transactions
@UseCase
public class PlaceOrderUseCase {
    // @Transactional is inherited from @UseCase
    public Order execute(OrderRequest request) { ... }
}
```

The `@UseCase` annotation is discovered by component-scan (inherits from `@Service` which inherits from `@Component`), and every class annotated with it automatically gets `@Transactional` behavior. This pattern is common in **hexagonal architecture** codebases where the port/adapter boundary is enforced through custom stereotypes rather than package naming conventions.

`@SpringBootApplication` is a composed annotation that includes `@ComponentScan` with no explicit `basePackages` — which means it defaults to **the package of the class it's on**. This is why the Spring Boot convention is to place your main class in the root package of your project.

```java
// com.example.app.Application — scans com.example.app and all sub-packages
package com.example.app;

@SpringBootApplication
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
}
```

With this structure, classes in `com.example.app.service`, `com.example.app.controller`, and `com.example.app.repository` are all discovered automatically. But `com.example.shared.utils` is outside the boundary — it won't be scanned.

To extend the scan, you can add explicit base packages. But be precise — scanning too broadly (like `com.example`) pulls in third-party library classes that happen to be annotated with `@Component`, which causes unexpected bean conflicts and slower startup.
